  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, and welcome. I'm Dr. [Name], a psychiatrist here. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S009",
      "patient_response": "Well, Doc, to be completely honest with you, it’s... well, I just keep forgetting things that I really, really shouldn't be forgetting. I mean, I know I'm not a spring chicken anymore, but this is just ridiculous. Like, for example, my heart follow-up appointments. You'd think a guy would remember something as important as checking on his own ticker, right? But the day just comes and goes, and I completely blow right past it because I'm off doing something else, just moving too fast, I guess. \n\nAnd then the clinic will call and leave a message, and I'll listen to it and tell myself, 'Okay, call the clinic back right this second,' but then I walk into the kitchen to grab a glass of water and... poof. The thought is just completely gone. I never call them back. It's like my brain just changes the channel. \n\nAnd it’s not just the medical stuff, it's my day-to-day life. I like to keep busy, you know, doing little repair jobs around the house or helping out the neighbors since I'm not working anymore. But I can't tell you how many times I'll get all the way over to a job, ready to get my hands dirty, and I look down and realize I didn't bring the right tools. I'll show up to fix a pipe and realize I left the main wrench sitting right on the kitchen counter, or I'll have the drill but I left all the bits sitting out in the garage. I have to walk back and forth three or four times just to start a simple job. It’s driving me absolutely up the wall, and I just... I don't know, it's frustrating, you know? It's just a constant cycle of forgetting the most obvious things.",
