    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Besides the forgetfulness and trouble focusing, have you noticed any problems with confusion, getting lost in familiar places, or difficulty finding words when you're talking?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "No, no, nothing like that at all. I mean, I don't get confused about where I am or anything. I've lived around these parts for, gosh, must be over forty years now, and I know every single street, every back alley, every shortcut. Just the other day, I was driving back from the hardware store—making my third trip, like I told you—and I took the old dirt road behind the elementary school just to avoid the traffic light, and I knew exactly where I was the whole time. My internal compass is perfectly fine. I never get lost. \n\nAnd as for finding words... well, if you ask anybody who knows me, they'll tell you I have the exact opposite problem! I don't lose my words, I probably use way too many of 'em! My brain is always just going, going, going, so sometimes I might, you know, trip over my own tongue a little bit because I'm trying to say three things at once, but the words are definitely all up there. I know exactly what I want to say, I just want to say it all right this second. \n\nSo, no, I'm sharp as a tack when it comes to knowing what's going on around me, who people are, what year it is, all that standard stuff. I'm not confused. It’s really just... it’s the stopping to remember a specific detail, like a phone call or a wrench. If I’m moving and talking, I’m totally clear-headed. It's just the memory for those little everyday things that slip right through the cracks because I'm already rushing on to the next thought.",
