      "doctor_question": "Before that cardiac scare fourteen months ago, would you say you had always been someone who was naturally forgetful about details, or was your memory for things like appointments and bringing tools pretty reliable back then?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, you know, it’s a really interesting question, Doc, because if you look at it one way... no, my memory for things like bringing the right wrench or making it to an appointment was actually pretty darn good back before the heart scare. But you have to understand the whole picture, you have to understand *why* it was fine back then. It wasn't because I was sitting around making tidy little checklists, I can tell you that for free. It was because I was constantly in motion! I had a rhythm to my day. As long as I was moving, as long as I was working on something physical, I was totally fine. \n\nBut if you're asking if my brain has always been this... this wound up? Oh, absolutely. Gosh, it goes way, way back. I mean, even as a boy in grade school, I was always that kid. You know the one. I was the kid who just absolutely could not stay in his seat to save his life. The teachers would be up at the chalkboard trying to get through a math problem or a history lesson, and I’d just blurt out the answers before they even finished asking the question. I just couldn't hold it in! It was like the thought was too big for my head and had to come out my mouth right that second. \n\nAnd my poor mother, bless her heart, she was always having to deal with the fallout. I got so many notes sent home for talking too much, interrupting the other kids, just being a general chatterbox. The teachers would write, 'He's a bright boy, but he just won't stop talking and disrupting the class.' \n\nSo, you see, I've always had this... this motor running inside me, ever since I was a little guy. For decades and decades, I just managed it by working hard, staying on my feet, doing heavy physical labor. If I was out building a fence or tearing down a shed, I didn't forget my tools because my body and my brain were finally moving at the exact same speed. I was burning off the restless energy. But now? Now that the heart doctors made me hit the brakes and sit around? It's like that little kid who couldn't stay in his seat is back, except now he's 68 years old, he's supposed to be resting, and his brain is just spinning out of control and dropping all the important details because it doesn't have anywhere else to put all that energy.",
